# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Longer life stories of the narrating Companions (online edition, 'Read more').

Each story: list of paragraphs, then 'lessons' — what we learn from the life.
"""

STORIES = {
"umar": dict(paras=[
"Before Islam, ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb was one of the strongest and most feared men of Quraysh. He was tall, a skilled wrestler and horseman, one of the few in Makkah who could read and write, and he was sent by Quraysh as their ambassador. He hated the new religion and harmed the Muslims. The Prophet ﷺ prayed: “O Allah, strengthen Islam with whichever of these two men is more beloved to You: Abū Jahl or ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb.”",
"One day ʿUmar set out with his sword, intending to kill the Prophet ﷺ. On the way he was told that his own sister Fāṭimah and her husband had become Muslim. He rushed to their house and heard them reciting. In anger he struck his sister until she bled — yet she stood firm and said: “Do what you like; we have accepted Islam.” Ashamed, he asked to see what they were reading. It was the beginning of Sūrah Ṭā-Hā. As he read, his heart softened, and he said: “How beautiful and noble these words are!” He went straight to the Prophet ﷺ and accepted Islam. The Muslims shouted ‘Allāhu akbar’ so loudly that it was heard across Makkah.",
"After he became Muslim, the believers prayed openly at the Kaʿbah for the first time. When others emigrated to Madinah in secret, ʿUmar is reported to have gone openly and challenged anyone who wanted to stop him. He fought at Badr, Uḥud and every battle. Several times the Qur’an was revealed in agreement with his opinion — about the station of Ibrāhīm, the ḥijāb, and the prisoners of Badr. The Prophet ﷺ said: “If there were to be a prophet after me, it would have been ʿUmar,” and: “Whenever Shayṭān sees you on a path, he takes a different path.”",
"When Abū Bakr died, ʿUmar became the second caliph (13–23 AH). He ruled the largest state of his time, yet he wore patched clothes, slept under trees, and walked the streets of Madinah at night to check on the people. One night he heard children crying from hunger in a tent; he carried a sack of flour on his own back and cooked for them himself, saying: “Who will carry my burdens for me on the Day of Judgement?” He set up the Hijri calendar, salaries for soldiers and the poor, and courts of justice. During the famine of the Year of Ashes he refused to eat meat or butter until the people could.",
"He prayed for martyrdom in the city of the Prophet ﷺ, and Allah granted it: he was stabbed while leading the Fajr prayer in 23 AH. Before he died he asked ʿĀ’ishah for permission to be buried beside his two companions, and she gave it. He lies today next to the Prophet ﷺ and Abū Bakr."],
lessons=["No one is too far for Allah to guide: the enemy of Islam became one of its greatest heroes.",
"The Qur’an can change a heart in a single reading.",
"True leadership is service: the ruler carried flour on his back for hungry children.",
"Justice and humility make a person great, not wealth or power."]),

"ibnumar": dict(paras=[
"ʿAbdullāh, the son of ʿUmar, accepted Islam as a young boy in Makkah, together with his father, and emigrated to Madinah before him. At thirteen he presented himself to fight at Badr and again at Uḥud, but the Prophet ﷺ sent him back because he was too young. At fifteen he was accepted for the Battle of the Trench, and after that he took part in many battles and in the Pledge of Riḍwān.",
"Ibn ʿUmar was known above all for following the Prophet ﷺ in every detail. On journeys he would stop where the Prophet ﷺ had stopped, pray where he had prayed, and even let his camel turn where the Prophet’s ﷺ camel had turned. Once his sister Ḥafṣah told the Prophet ﷺ about a dream ʿAbdullāh had seen, and the Prophet ﷺ said: “What an excellent man ʿAbdullāh is, if only he prayed at night.” From that day he slept very little of the night.",
"He was generous: whenever something he owned pleased him greatly, he gave it away in charity, recalling the verse “You will never attain righteousness until you spend from what you love.” His servants noticed this, and some would act very pious to be freed; when he was told, he said: “Whoever deceives us for the sake of Allah, we let ourselves be deceived.”",
"During the civil strife after ʿUthmān, many people wanted Ibn ʿUmar to be caliph, but he refused to fight Muslims for power and kept away from the fighting. He spent his long life teaching. He narrated over 2,600 hadith and gave fatwas for about sixty years. He was one of the last great Companions in Makkah, where he died in 73 AH, aged over eighty."],
lessons=["Love of the Sunnah shows in small details as well as big ones.",
"Accept advice at once: one sentence from the Prophet ﷺ changed his nights for life.",
"Give from what you love most.",
"Refusing power to protect Muslim blood is true wisdom."]),

"ibnmasud": dict(paras=[
"ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd was a poor, thin young shepherd in Makkah who looked after the sheep of ʿUqbah ibn Abī Muʿayṭ. One day the Prophet ﷺ and Abū Bakr passed by and asked for milk. He said: “I am trusted with them; I cannot.” The Prophet ﷺ took a young ewe that had never given milk, touched her udder, and milk flowed. Ibn Masʿūd said: “Teach me these words,” and the Prophet ﷺ said: “You are a young man who will be taught.” He became the sixth person to accept Islam.",
"The Companions said that the Quraysh had never heard the Qur’an recited openly at the Kaʿbah. Ibn Masʿūd, though small and without a strong tribe, volunteered. He stood by the Kaʿbah and recited Sūrah ar-Raḥmān aloud. The Quraysh beat him until his face was bruised. When his friends said “This is what we feared,” he replied: “The enemies of Allah were never easier for me than today; if you wish, I will do it again tomorrow.”",
"He became the Prophet’s ﷺ close servant, carrying his sandals, his siwāk and his water, and entering his house so often that strangers thought he was family. He learned about seventy sūrahs directly from the Prophet’s ﷺ mouth. Once the Prophet ﷺ asked him to recite to him; he recited from an-Nisā’ until he reached “How will it be when We bring from every nation a witness, and bring you as a witness over these?” and the Prophet ﷺ said “Enough,” with tears in his eyes. His legs were very thin, and when some laughed at them the Prophet ﷺ said: “They are heavier on the Scale than Mount Uḥud.”",
"ʿUmar sent him to Kūfah as a teacher and treasurer, writing: “I have preferred you over myself with him.” The great school of fiqh of Kūfah came from his students. He died in Madinah in 32 AH, aged about sixty."],
lessons=["Honesty with what you are trusted with opens doors.",
"Courage is not about size or tribe but about faith.",
"Learn the Qur’an from its people and live with it.",
"People are weighed by their deeds, not their appearance."]),

"aishah": dict(paras=[
"ʿĀ’ishah was the daughter of Abū Bakr aṣ-Ṣiddīq and Umm Rūmān, born into Islam. She married the Prophet ﷺ and lived with him in a small room next to the mosque in Madinah. When he was asked who was most beloved to him among people, he said: “ʿĀ’ishah,” and among men: “Her father.” Revelation came to him while he was under her blanket, which did not happen with any other wife.",
"She was tested with a great slander: hypocrites spread a lie about her honour after an expedition. For a month she suffered and wept, until Allah revealed ten verses of Sūrah an-Nūr declaring her innocence. Her mother told her to thank the Prophet ﷺ, and she said: “By Allah, I will only thank Allah,” showing that her trust was in her Lord.",
"She had an extraordinary memory and understanding. She knew the Qur’an, hadith, fiqh, inheritance, Arabic poetry, the history of the Arabs and even medicine. Senior Companions came to her with difficult questions, and Abū Mūsā said: “Whenever we Companions found a hadith difficult, we asked ʿĀ’ishah and found she had knowledge of it.” She narrated over 2,200 hadith, many about the Prophet’s ﷺ life at home: his worship at night, his manners with his family, his character, which she described in one sentence: “His character was the Qur’an.”",
"She was very generous. Once she received a hundred thousand dirhams and gave it all away the same day while fasting; when evening came she had nothing left for her own iftar. The Prophet ﷺ died in her room with his head on her chest, and he is buried there. She lived on teaching the Ummah for nearly fifty years and died in Madinah in 57 AH."],
lessons=["Women carried and taught a huge part of the Sunnah.",
"Patience under false accusation is rewarded, and trust belongs to Allah alone.",
"Knowledge is a lifelong service to the Ummah.",
"Generosity means forgetting yourself for others."]),

"numan": dict(paras=[
"An-Nuʿmān was the first child born to the Anṣār after the Hijrah, fourteen months after the Prophet ﷺ arrived in Madinah, and the Muslims rejoiced at his birth. His father Bashīr ibn Saʿd and his mother ʿAmrah were Companions, and his uncle was the famous ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah.",
"An-Nuʿmān was about eight years old when the Prophet ﷺ died, yet he narrated several important hadith. When he narrated ‘The halal is clear…’ he pointed with his fingers to his ears and said: “I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say…” to show he heard it himself.",
"One of his famous memories is about a gift. His father wanted to give him a special gift and asked the Prophet ﷺ to witness it. The Prophet ﷺ asked: “Have you given the same to all your children?” He said no. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Fear Allah and be just between your children,” and refused to witness an injustice. So his father took the gift back.",
"As an adult he was a poet, a judge and a governor of Kūfah and later Ḥimṣ, known for his gentleness and patience with people. He was killed in the troubles of 65 AH."],
lessons=["Children can learn and pass on the religion.",
"Parents must be fair between their children.",
"Gentleness is a strength in leaders."]),

"tamim": dict(paras=[
"Tamīm ibn Aws ad-Dārī was a Christian from Palestine, a worshipper who had studied the scriptures. In 9 AH he came to Madinah with his brother and accepted Islam.",
"He told the Prophet ﷺ an amazing story: on a sea journey, his ship was blown off course for a month to an island, where he met a strange creature and a chained man who asked about the Prophet ﷺ and said he was the Dajjāl. The Prophet ﷺ gathered the people and narrated Tamīm’s story to them from the pulpit, saying it agreed with what he had told them about the Dajjāl. It is rare that the Prophet ﷺ narrated something from one of his Companions.",
"Tamīm was famous for his worship. He would sometimes stand the whole night reciting the Qur’an, and it is reported he once spent a whole night repeating one verse: “Or do those who commit evil deeds think We will make them like those who believe and do righteous deeds?” (al-Jāthiyah 45:21). He is reported to be the first to light lamps in the Prophet’s Mosque, and the Prophet ﷺ prayed for him: “You have lit up our mosque; may Allah light you up.”",
"ʿUmar appointed him to lead the people in the night prayer of Ramadan together with Ubayy ibn Kaʿb. He later returned to Palestine and died there around 40 AH."],
lessons=["Sincere seekers of truth are guided to it.",
"Small services to the mosque, like lighting it, are loved by Allah.",
"Reflecting deeply on one verse can be worth a whole night."]),

"abuhurayrah": dict(paras=[
"Abū Hurayrah came from the tribe of Daws in Yemen. His tribe accepted Islam through aṭ-Ṭufayl ibn ʿAmr, and in 7 AH Abū Hurayrah arrived in Madinah while the Prophet ﷺ was at Khaybar. He was called ‘Abū Hurayrah’ — father of the little cat — because he used to carry a kitten with him when he looked after his family’s sheep.",
"He had no trade or farm, so he chose to stay with the Prophet ﷺ day and night, living among the poor in the Ṣuffah of the mosque. He said: “My brothers among the Muhājirūn were busy in the markets, and my brothers among the Anṣār were busy with their wealth, while I stayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with only enough to fill my stomach.” He was often so hungry that he fainted. Once the Prophet ﷺ saw his hunger and gave him a single cup of milk to call all the people of the Ṣuffah; they all drank, and then the Prophet ﷺ made Abū Hurayrah drink until he said “I cannot drink more.”",
"He complained that he forgot things, so the Prophet ﷺ told him to spread out his cloak, spoke to him, and told him to gather it to his chest. He said: “After that I never forgot anything he told me.” This is how he narrated over 5,000 hadith in only about four years of companionship. Marwān once tested him by secretly writing down hadith he narrated, then asking him again a year later: he did not miss a word.",
"His mother refused Islam and once said something hurtful about the Prophet ﷺ. Abū Hurayrah came weeping and asked him to pray for her. The Prophet ﷺ prayed, and when he returned home, he heard water: his mother had made ghusl and said the shahādah. Abū Hurayrah wept with joy. He later served as governor of Madinah, yet he carried firewood on his back through the market saying, “Make way for the governor!” He died in Madinah in 57 AH."],
lessons=["Sacrifice for knowledge is rewarded with blessing.",
"Never give up making du‘a for your parents.",
"A short time with sincerity can produce a lifetime of benefit.",
"Humility suits those in authority."]),

"hasan": dict(paras=[
"Al-Ḥasan was born in the third year after the Hijrah to ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib and Fāṭimah, the daughter of the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ named him al-Ḥasan, ‘the beautiful’, gave him his first taste of dates, and gave silver equal to the weight of his hair in charity. Al-Ḥasan resembled his grandfather more than anyone.",
"The Prophet ﷺ loved him deeply. He would carry him on his shoulders and say: “O Allah, I love him, so love him.” Once the Prophet ﷺ was giving a khuṭbah when al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn came walking and stumbling in red shirts; he came down from the pulpit, picked them up and said: “Allah spoke the truth: your wealth and your children are a test.” Sometimes the boys climbed on his back while he was in sujūd, and he lengthened the prostration so as not to disturb them.",
"The Prophet ﷺ foretold a great event: “This son of mine is a master (sayyid), and perhaps Allah will make peace through him between two great groups of Muslims.” Thirty years later, after his father ʿAlī was killed, al-Ḥasan was given the pledge as caliph and had a large army. But to stop Muslims killing Muslims, he handed over leadership to Muʿāwiyah in 41 AH. That year was called ‘the Year of Unity’.",
"He was known for generosity, gentleness and forbearance. He performed Hajj many times walking, and twice gave away all his wealth for Allah. He died in Madinah in 49 or 50 AH and is buried in al-Baqīʿ."],
lessons=["Giving up a worldly right for the unity of Muslims is greatness.",
"The Prophet ﷺ showed love and mercy to children.",
"Generosity and gentleness are the marks of noble character."]),

"anas": dict(paras=[
"When the Prophet ﷺ arrived in Madinah, the Anṣār brought him gifts. Umm Sulaym had no wealth to give, so she brought her ten-year-old son Anas and said: “O Messenger of Allah, this is Anas; he will serve you.” From that day Anas served the Prophet ﷺ for ten years, at home and on journeys.",
"He described those years with amazement: “I served him for ten years, and he never once said to me ‘uff’, nor said about something I did, ‘Why did you do that?’, nor about something I left, ‘Why did you not do it?’” Once the Prophet ﷺ sent him on an errand, and he went off to play with boys in the market instead. The Prophet ﷺ came, took him gently by the back of his neck, and smiled, saying: “Little Anas, did you go where I told you?” He replied: “Yes, I am going now, O Messenger of Allah!”",
"Umm Sulaym asked the Prophet ﷺ to pray for her son, and he said: “O Allah, increase his wealth and children and bless him in what You have given him.” Anas lived to see more than a hundred of his children and grandchildren, and his orchard gave fruit twice a year.",
"He narrated over 2,200 hadith, describing the Prophet’s ﷺ hands softer than silk, his scent better than musk, and his daily habits. He lived long, teaching in Basra, and was one of the last Companions to die there in 93 AH. He said: “There is no night that passes but I see my beloved (the Prophet ﷺ) in my dreams,” and he wept."],
lessons=["Serving others with love is honourable.",
"The Prophet ﷺ was patient and kind with children and servants.",
"The du‘a of a righteous person for a child can bless a whole life."]),

"shaddad": dict(paras=[
"Shaddād ibn Aws was from the Banū an-Najjār of the Anṣār in Madinah, the son of Aws ibn Thābit, who fought at Badr and was martyred at Uḥud. His uncle was Ḥassān ibn Thābit, the poet who defended the Prophet ﷺ with his verses.",
"Shaddād was known for knowledge and careful speech. Abū ad-Dardā’ said: “Every nation has a man of understanding, and the man of understanding of this nation is Shaddād ibn Aws.” His companions said that when he spoke, he did not speak a word without weighing it. Once he said something careless and immediately corrected himself, saying that since accepting Islam he had not spoken a word without first thinking about it.",
"He was also known for his worship. When he lay down on his bed at night he would turn from side to side, unable to sleep from fear of the Fire, then get up and pray until dawn.",
"He settled in Palestine and taught there. Many of the people of Shām learned from him, and he died there in 58 AH. The hadith on iḥsān — doing everything with excellence — is his most famous narration."],
lessons=["Weigh your words before speaking.",
"Knowledge and worship go together.",
"Excellence (iḥsān) is a way of life, not only a lesson."]),

"abudharr": dict(paras=[
"Abū Dharr Jundub ibn Junādah came from the tribe of Ghifār, a desert tribe known as highway robbers. But Abū Dharr hated idols and used to worship Allah alone in his own way, even before he heard of Islam.",
"When news reached him of a prophet in Makkah, he sent his brother to find out, then went himself. He stayed hidden near the Kaʿbah for days, drinking only Zamzam, until ʿAlī brought him secretly to the Prophet ﷺ. He accepted Islam at once — one of the first five people. The Prophet ﷺ told him to keep it secret and return to his people, but he said: “By the One who sent you with the truth, I will shout it among them!” He went to the Kaʿbah and declared the shahādah loudly. The Quraysh beat him until al-ʿAbbās saved him by reminding them that Ghifār lay on their trade route. The next day he did it again.",
"He returned home and called his tribe; half of Ghifār accepted Islam, and the rest followed later, along with the tribe of Aslam. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Ghifār — may Allah forgive them; Aslam — may Allah make them safe.” He also said: “The sky has not shaded, nor the earth carried, anyone more truthful in speech than Abū Dharr.”",
"Abū Dharr was strict with himself in zuhd and spoke harshly against hoarding wealth. The Prophet ﷺ had told him he would live alone, die alone and be raised alone. Late in life he lived in the desert at ar-Rabadhah, and he died there in 32 AH with only his wife and servant. A caravan led by ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd passed by just in time to bury him; Ibn Masʿūd wept, remembering the Prophet’s ﷺ words."],
lessons=["The fiṭrah can lead a person to tawḥīd even before he hears of Islam.",
"Courage in declaring the truth can guide whole tribes.",
"Truthfulness is a crown of character."]),

"muadh": dict(paras=[
"Muʿādh ibn Jabal accepted Islam in Madinah as a teenager, through Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr, the first teacher sent from Makkah. He was among the seventy Anṣār who gave the Second Pledge at ʿAqabah, and he fought at Badr when he was about twenty, and in every battle after.",
"He was handsome, generous and full of knowledge. He was one of the few who gathered the whole Qur’an in the Prophet’s ﷺ lifetime. The Prophet ﷺ said: “The most knowledgeable of my Ummah about the halal and the haram is Muʿādh ibn Jabal,” and that on the Day of Judgement he will come ahead of the scholars by a stone’s throw. Once the Prophet ﷺ took his hand and said: “O Muʿādh, by Allah I love you. Never leave saying after every prayer: O Allah, help me to remember You, thank You and worship You well.”",
"The Prophet ﷺ sent him to Yemen as a teacher and judge. When he asked how he would judge, Muʿādh replied: by the Book of Allah, then the Sunnah, then by his own reasoning — and the Prophet ﷺ was pleased. When he left, the Prophet ﷺ walked beside his camel giving him advice, and said: “O Muʿādh, perhaps you will not meet me after this year,” and Muʿādh wept. It was so: the Prophet ﷺ passed away while he was in Yemen.",
"Under ʿUmar he went to Shām to teach. When the plague of ʿAmwās struck in 18 AH, he did not run from it; he said it was a mercy and martyrdom, and he died of it at about thirty-three years old. ʿUmar said: “If I had appointed Muʿādh as my successor and Allah asked me, I would say: I heard Your Prophet say he is the most knowledgeable of the halal and the haram.”"],
lessons=["Youth is the best time to seek knowledge.",
"The love of the Prophet ﷺ for his Companions was expressed in words.",
"Knowledge brings great rank with Allah.",
"Judge by the Qur’an first, then the Sunnah."]),

"ibnabbas": dict(paras=[
"ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās was the son of the Prophet’s ﷺ uncle al-ʿAbbās, born in Makkah three years before the Hijrah. As a boy he often stayed at the house of his aunt Maymūnah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ. One night he watched the Prophet ﷺ pray at night and joined him; another time he put out water for the Prophet’s ﷺ wudu without being asked. The Prophet ﷺ was so pleased that he hugged him and prayed: “O Allah, give him understanding of the religion and teach him the interpretation (of the Qur’an).”",
"He was about thirteen when the Prophet ﷺ died. Instead of thinking ‘the chance has gone’, he said to an Anṣārī friend: “Let us go and ask the Companions, for they are many today.” His friend said: “Do you think people will need you while these Companions are alive?” But Ibn ʿAbbās went from house to house. Sometimes he came at midday and found a Companion resting, so he lay down at his door with his cloak as a pillow while the wind blew dust over him, rather than wake him. When the Companion came out and said “Why did you not send for me?”, he said: “I am more worthy to come to you.” Years later that same friend saw people crowding around Ibn ʿAbbās to learn, and said: “This young man was wiser than me.”",
"ʿUmar used to bring the young Ibn ʿAbbās into his council with the elders of Badr. When some objected, ʿUmar asked them the meaning of Sūrah an-Naṣr; only Ibn ʿAbbās understood that it announced the nearness of the Prophet’s ﷺ death. He became known as Ḥibr al-Ummah, the scholar of the nation, and Turjumān al-Qur’ān, the interpreter of the Qur’an. His lessons were organised by day: one day tafsīr, one day fiqh, one day the battles, one day poetry, one day the history of the Arabs.",
"In old age he became blind, and he died in aṭ-Ṭā’if in 68 AH, about seventy years old."],
lessons=["Serve the people of knowledge and be patient in learning.",
"It is never too late to start learning.",
"Humility before teachers opens the doors of knowledge.",
"The du‘a of the Prophet ﷺ was answered in a child."]),

"abumasud": dict(paras=[
"ʿUqbah ibn ʿAmr, known as Abū Masʿūd, was from the Anṣār of Madinah. He was one of the youngest of the seventy who met the Prophet ﷺ secretly at night at ʿAqabah and gave the pledge to protect him. He was named ‘al-Badrī’ because he lived near the well of Badr.",
"Abū Masʿūd later recalled a moment that changed him. He was beating his slave boy with a whip when he heard a voice behind him: “Know, Abū Masʿūd!” He was too angry to recognise it until the speaker came close: it was the Prophet ﷺ, saying: “Know, Abū Masʿūd, that Allah has more power over you than you have over this boy.” The whip fell from his hand. He said: “He is free for the sake of Allah!” The Prophet ﷺ said: “If you had not done so, the Fire would have touched you.”",
"He fought at Uḥud and the battles after it, and later settled in Kūfah, where ʿAlī once left him in charge when he went out. He narrated about a hundred hadith, including the one on modesty in this book. He died around 40 AH."],
lessons=["Remember Allah’s power over you when you have power over others.",
"Correct a wrong immediately and make amends.",
"Modesty and mercy belong together."]),

"sufyan": dict(paras=[
"Sufyān ibn ʿAbdullāh came from Thaqīf, the proud tribe of aṭ-Ṭā’if. This was the same city that had thrown stones at the Prophet ﷺ when he came to call them to Islam years earlier, and later resisted a long siege after the Battle of Ḥunayn.",
"In 9 AH, Thaqīf finally sent a delegation to Madinah and accepted Islam, and Sufyān was among them. Like many sincere new Muslims, he wanted one clear, complete instruction he could hold on to, so he asked: “O Messenger of Allah, tell me something about Islam that I will not need to ask anyone else after you.” The answer — ‘Say: I believe in Allah, then be steadfast’ — became one of the most comprehensive sayings in Islam. In another narration he asked: “What do you fear most for me?” and the Prophet ﷺ took hold of his own tongue and said: “This.”",
"ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb later trusted him with collecting the zakah of aṭ-Ṭā’if, a sign of his honesty."],
lessons=["Ask for knowledge that changes your life, not for argument.",
"Steadfastness is the fruit of true faith.",
"The city that once rejected the Prophet ﷺ became Muslim: never give up on people."]),

"jabir": dict(paras=[
"Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh was a young Anṣārī who came with his father to the Second Pledge of ʿAqabah. His father, ʿAbdullāh, was martyred at Uḥud. The Prophet ﷺ told Jābir that Allah spoke to his father directly, without a veil, and offered him anything he wished; he asked to return to the world to be killed again for Allah.",
"His father left nine daughters and many debts. Jābir chose to marry a mature, previously married woman so she could care for his young sisters, and the Prophet ﷺ praised his choice. When the creditors demanded payment, the Prophet ﷺ walked around Jābir’s heap of dates and sat on it; all the debts were paid and the dates remained as if untouched.",
"During the digging of the Trench, Jābir saw the Prophet ﷺ tying a stone to his stomach from hunger. He hurried home, slaughtered a small goat, and his wife made a little bread, and he quietly invited the Prophet ﷺ with a few men. Instead, the Prophet ﷺ called out to all the diggers — about a thousand — and blessed the food. All of them ate until full, and the pot and dough were still not finished.",
"Jābir took part in nineteen expeditions and the Pledge of Riḍwān. His love for knowledge was so strong that he travelled a month to Shām to hear a single hadith from ʿAbdullāh ibn Unays. He narrated over 1,500 hadith, taught in the Prophet’s Mosque, and was among the last Companions to die in Madinah, in 78 AH."],
lessons=["Care for your family is part of faith.",
"Share what little you have; Allah puts blessing in it.",
"Travel and effort for knowledge are worth it."]),

"abumalik": dict(paras=[
"Abū Mālik al-Ashʿarī belonged to the Ashʿarīs, a tribe from Yemen who sailed to the Prophet ﷺ and arrived at the time of Khaybar. The Prophet ﷺ praised them: “The people of Yemen have come to you; they are the softest of hearts. Faith is Yemeni and wisdom is Yemeni.”",
"He also praised the Ashʿarīs for their brotherhood: “When the Ashʿarīs run short of food on a journey or at home, they gather all they have in one cloth and divide it equally among themselves. They are from me and I am from them.”",
"Abū Mālik narrated a number of important hadith, including the one in this book, “Purity is half of faith”. He settled in Shām with the Muslim armies and died in the great plague of ʿAmwās in 18 AH, the same plague that took Muʿādh ibn Jabal and Abū ʿUbaydah."],
lessons=["Soft hearts are quick to accept the truth.",
"Sharing equally in hardship builds a loving community.",
"A short hadith can carry a whole way of life."]),

"nawwas": dict(paras=[
"An-Nawwās ibn Samʿān came from the tribe of Kilāb. He wanted so much to learn that he stayed in Madinah for a whole year, not as an emigrant but only to be able to ask the Prophet ﷺ questions. He explained: if he became an emigrant, he would be shy to ask too much, as the emigrants were; as a visitor he could keep asking.",
"His curiosity gave the Ummah great knowledge. He narrated the famous hadith of the Dajjāl: how long he will remain, how he will try people, how ʿĪsā ibn Maryam will descend by the white minaret east of Damascus and kill him, and about Gog and Magog. He also narrated the definition of righteousness and sin in this book.",
"He later settled in Shām."],
lessons=["Make time and sacrifice to learn your religion.",
"Good questions bring benefit to everyone.",
"Knowledge of the future signs protects the believer from trials."]),

"wabisah": dict(paras=[
"Wābiṣah ibn Maʿbad came to the Prophet ﷺ in 9 AH with the delegation of his tribe, Banū Asad. He had decided to ask about every kind of good and every kind of evil, leaving nothing out.",
"He pushed through the crowd to get close, and the Companions told him to keep back, but the Prophet ﷺ said: “Let Wābiṣah come near.” When he sat down close, before he could speak, the Prophet ﷺ said: “Shall I tell you what you have come to ask, or will you ask?” He said: “Tell me.” The Prophet ﷺ said: “You have come to ask about righteousness and sin.” Then he gathered his fingers, tapped them on Wābiṣah’s chest, and said: “Consult your heart, consult yourself…”",
"Wābiṣah was known for worship and weeping from the fear of Allah. He settled in Kūfah and later ar-Raqqah on the Euphrates, where he died."],
lessons=["Come to learning with determination.",
"A living heart is a guide in unclear matters.",
"The Prophet ﷺ welcomed sincere seekers warmly."]),

"irbad": dict(paras=[
"Al-ʿIrbāḍ ibn Sāriyah was from the tribe of Sulaym and one of the people of the Ṣuffah, the poor Companions who lived in the mosque with no home or wealth, devoting themselves to learning and worship.",
"When the Muslims prepared for the long expedition to Tabūk, some poor Companions had no camel to ride and could not join. They came to the Prophet ﷺ, and he had nothing to give them, so they turned away with their eyes overflowing with tears, sad that they could not spend in Allah’s path. Allah revealed about them: “Nor upon those who came to you to be given mounts and you said, ‘I can find nothing to carry you upon,’ and they turned back while their eyes overflowed with tears…” (at-Tawbah 9:92). Al-ʿIrbāḍ was one of them.",
"He narrated the famous farewell sermon in this book. In old age he settled in Ḥimṣ in Shām. It is reported that he used to pray: “O Allah, my age has grown and my bones have weakened, so take me to You.” He died there in 75 AH."],
lessons=["Sincere intention is rewarded even when ability is lacking.",
"Wealth is not the measure of a believer.",
"Remember the Prophet’s ﷺ advice: hold to his Sunnah."]),

"abuthalabah": dict(paras=[
"Abū Thaʿlabah al-Khushanī, Jurthūm ibn Nāshir, was from the tribe of Khushayn. He gave the Pledge of Riḍwān under the tree at al-Ḥudaybiyah in 6 AH, one of those about whom Allah said: “Allah was pleased with the believers when they pledged to you under the tree” (al-Fatḥ 48:18).",
"The Prophet ﷺ sent him back to call his own people to Islam, and they accepted. He loved to ask about the halal and the haram, especially about hunting, vessels and food, and he narrated a number of rulings about them.",
"He settled in Shām. He used to say: “I hope that Allah will not make me suffer at death as I see others suffer.” One night, while he was praying in the middle of the night, he died in sujūd. His daughter saw him in prostration and thought he was still praying; when he did not move, she found that his soul had been taken. This was in 75 AH."],
lessons=["Those who live upon something often die upon it.",
"Calling one’s own people to Islam is a great service.",
"Ask about the rulings you need in daily life."]),

"sahl": dict(paras=[
"Sahl ibn Saʿd as-Sāʿidī was from the Anṣār of Madinah. He was named Ḥazn, which means ‘sadness’ or ‘rough ground’, but the Prophet ﷺ changed his name to Sahl, meaning ‘ease’. He was about fifteen years old when the Prophet ﷺ passed away.",
"He narrated many beautiful hadith, including the story of the man who wanted to marry but had nothing to give, not even an iron ring, and the Prophet ﷺ married him for what he knew of the Qur’an. He also narrated that in Paradise there is a gate called ar-Rayyān, through which only those who fasted will enter.",
"Sahl lived a long life. Under the tyranny of al-Ḥajjāj he was humiliated for his loyalty to ʿUthmān, but he remained patient. He is said to be the last Companion to die in Madinah, in 91 AH, at about a hundred years old."],
lessons=["Good names matter; choose names with good meanings.",
"The Qur’an is the greatest wealth.",
"Patience under oppression is honourable."]),

"abusaid": dict(paras=[
"Saʿd ibn Mālik ibn Sinān, known as Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, was from the Anṣār. At thirteen, on the day of Uḥud, his father brought him to the Prophet ﷺ and said: “He has strong bones,” but the Prophet ﷺ sent him back because he was too young. His father was martyred at Uḥud, leaving the family poor.",
"Once, very hungry, he went to ask the Prophet ﷺ for help, and arrived as he was saying: “Whoever seeks to be self-sufficient, Allah makes him self-sufficient; whoever seeks to be content, Allah makes him content.” Abū Saʿīd turned back without asking, and later Allah gave him plenty.",
"He fought in twelve expeditions, starting with the Battle of the Trench. He became one of the young jurists among the Companions, and narrated over 1,100 hadith.",
"He spoke the truth to those in power: when Marwān, governor of Madinah, gave the Eid sermon before the prayer, Abū Saʿīd openly reminded him of the Sunnah, and narrated the hadith on changing an evil. He died in Madinah in 74 AH."],
lessons=["Contentment brings real wealth.",
"Speaking the truth wisely is part of faith.",
"Patience in poverty is rewarded."]),

"abdullahibnamr": dict(paras=[
"ʿAbdullāh, the son of the famous general ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, accepted Islam before his father. Abū Hurayrah said: “No one narrated more hadith than me except ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, because he used to write and I did not.” When some Quraysh told him not to write everything the Prophet ﷺ said, since he was a human who speaks in anger and in pleasure, the Prophet ﷺ pointed to his mouth and said: “Write, for by the One in whose hand is my soul, nothing comes out of it except the truth.” His notebook was called aṣ-Ṣaḥīfah aṣ-Ṣādiqah, ‘the truthful page’.",
"He was so devoted to worship that he fasted every day and prayed all night, reciting the whole Qur’an every night. His father complained to the Prophet ﷺ that his son was neglecting his wife. The Prophet ﷺ advised him: fast some days and break your fast on others; pray and also sleep; for your body, your eyes, your wife and your guests have a right over you. He bargained down to fasting every other day — the fast of Dāwūd — and reciting the Qur’an every seven days.",
"In old age he said: “I wish I had accepted the concession of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ,” because the heavy routine became hard, yet he did not want to leave something he had promised. He died in 65 AH."],
lessons=["Write down and preserve knowledge.",
"Balance in worship is the Sunnah: every right must be given.",
"Consistency in moderate deeds is better than extremes that cannot last."]),

"abumusa": dict(paras=[
"Abū Mūsā ʿAbdullāh ibn Qays al-Ashʿarī came from Yemen. He accepted Islam early and set sail with his people to meet the Prophet ﷺ, but the wind carried their ship to Abyssinia, where they met Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib and the Muslim emigrants. They all arrived together in Madinah at the time of Khaybar.",
"His voice in reciting the Qur’an was beautiful. One night the Prophet ﷺ stood listening to his recitation and the next day said: “If only you had seen me listening to your recitation last night! You have been given one of the flutes of the family of Dāwūd.” Abū Mūsā said: “If I had known you were listening, I would have beautified it even more for you.”",
"The Prophet ﷺ sent him with Muʿādh to Yemen with the advice: “Make things easy and do not make them difficult; give good news and do not drive people away; cooperate and do not differ.” Later ʿUmar appointed him governor of Basra, where he taught the people the Qur’an and fiqh, and of Kūfah. He played an important role in the conquests of Persia. He died around 44 AH."],
lessons=["Beautify your recitation of the Qur’an.",
"Make the religion easy for people and give good news.",
"Trials on the way (like the ship’s detour) can carry hidden good."]),

"miqdam": dict(paras=[
"Al-Miqdām ibn Maʿdī Karib came from the tribe of Kindah in Yemen, which sent a large delegation to the Prophet ﷺ in 10 AH, the ‘Year of Delegations’, when tribes from all over Arabia came to accept Islam.",
"He narrated several beautiful hadith, including: “No one has eaten better food than what he eats from the work of his own hands; the Prophet of Allah Dāwūd used to eat from the work of his hands,” and: “If a man loves his brother, let him tell him that he loves him.” His hadith on moderation in eating is in this book.",
"He settled in Ḥimṣ in Shām, where many of the Successors learned from him, and he died there in 87 AH, aged over ninety."],
lessons=["Earning with your own hands is honourable.",
"Tell your brothers you love them.",
"Moderation in food protects body and heart."]),

"ibnbusr": dict(paras=[
"ʿAbdullāh ibn Busr al-Māzinī came from a family of Companions: his father Busr, his mother, his brother and his sister all met the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ once visited their home; they gave him food and dates and a drink, and he made du‘a for them: “O Allah, bless them in what You have provided them, forgive them and have mercy on them.”",
"It is reported that, as a boy, ʿAbdullāh’s mother once sent him to the Prophet ﷺ with a bunch of grapes, but he ate them on the way. When the Prophet ﷺ later learned of it, he took him gently by the ear and said with a smile: “O deceiver!” He also put his hand on the boy’s head and said: “This boy will live a century.” He lived a hundred years, and the mole on his face that the Prophet ﷺ mentioned remained a sign.",
"He settled in Ḥimṣ and was among the last Companions to die in Shām, in 88 AH. The hadith on keeping the tongue moist with dhikr, which ends this book, is his."],
lessons=["A family can share faith together.",
"The Prophet ﷺ corrected children with humour and gentleness.",
"Make dhikr your lifelong companion."]),
}
